-Curve RMS is computed over all common accepted environment-0 times, normalized by the baseline RMS, with force components combined in newtons and moment treated separately in newton-metres. Temporal comparisons use the coarse accepted times shared with the finer run. Peak force and moment use every accepted sample in each run. Work is the integral of measured end-grip reaction moment against measured grip rotation; both signed external work and absolute path work are retained, and the 2% gate uses absolute path work. This is a numerical observable definition, not a claim that the path integral equals material dissipation.
+Curve RMS is computed over all common accepted environment-0 times, normalized by the baseline RMS, with force components combined in newtons and moment treated separately in newton-metres. Temporal comparisons use the coarse accepted times shared with the finer run. Peak force and moment use every accepted sample in each run. Work is the integral of measured end-grip reaction moment against measured grip rotation. Signed external work uses the trapezoidal integral. Absolute path work integrates the absolute value of the piecewise-linear moment on every measured-angle interval, splitting at an interior zero crossing when endpoint moments have opposite signs; this avoids cancellation that occurs if the absolute value is applied only after a signed trapezoid. Both signed and absolute work are retained, and the 2% gate uses absolute path work. This is a numerical observable definition, not a claim that the path integral equals material dissipation.
+
+The free-release change is measured from accepted pre-release and post-release endpoint grip angles. The v2 report retains the prior magnitude fields (`springback_angle_change_deg` and `springback_angle_difference_deg`) for compatibility and adds signed counterparts. The 1-degree gate uses `signed_springback_angle_difference_deg`; opposite-direction releases therefore cannot pass merely because their magnitudes match. Because v2 corrects the absolute-work integral and changes the springback gate, prior v1 convergence screens must be reevaluated before any promotion decision.
 
 Missing damage/debond fields are `inconclusive` when the corresponding mechanism is declared enabled. Feature enablement must match across a pair. No stage-resolution claim is made unless every run carries explicit native certificates for crease-tool contact, paper damage, and glue debond. The current end-grip coupon lacks these certificates and area observables, so even a successful mesh/timestep screen is labeled `numerical_screen_only_stage_scope_incomplete`. The current convergence loader also excludes the tool-driven crease mode, so this tool does not admit or qualify scoring-contact runs.
 
